[47.561513, "o", "  ⚙ read .\r\n    ✓ read ./ — 4 entries\r\n  ⚙ glob \r\n    ✓ glob **/pyproject.toml — 0 files\r\n  │ This is a system-level problem, not a repository one — and this session h…\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 3 tool call(s) so far\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 4 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ read .gitignore\r\n    ✓ read .gitignore — 8 lines\r\n  ⚙ read .gitattributes\r\n    ✓ read .gitattributes — 3 lines\r\nI couldn't do this one, and I want to be clear about why rather than hand you a confident guess.\r\n\r\n**What I checked:** the repository. It's empty apart from `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes` and the `.gnomon/` harness surface — no Python project, no `pyproject.toml`, no requirements file, nothing that touches pip.\r\n\r\n**Why I can't fix it from here:** two blockers, both hard.\r\n\r\n1. This session has no shell. I have `read`, `glob`, `grep`, `write`, `compute` — no `bash`, so I cannot run `pip`, `python3 -V`, or inspect your filesystem.\r\n2. Even with a shell, this sandbox is not your machine. The tool pat"]
[47.561553, "o", "hs resolve to this repository root and anything outside it is refused. Running `pip install` here would exercise the sandbox's interpreter, not your system-wide one, so the result would be theatre, not a diagnosis.\r\n\r\nSo there's nothing I can change on disk that would fix your installation, and I'm not going to write you an \"unverified fix script\" I was never able to run.\r\n\r\n**What it almost certainly is.** On any current Linux distro — Debian/Ubuntu 23.04+, Fedora 38+, Arch, and their derivatives — a system-wide `pip install` fails by design with `error: externally-managed-environment` (PEP 668). The interpreter is owned by the OS package manager, and pip now refuses to write into it. That's not a broken installation; it's the installation working as intended. The other two common causes look different and are distinguishable from the error text alone.\r\n\r\n**One command that separates them:**\r\n\r\n```\r\npython3 -m pip install --dry-run requests\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `externally-managed-environment` → PEP 668. Not brok"]
[47.561654, "o", "en. Create a venv (`python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate`) and install there; use `pipx` for CLI tools you want on `PATH`. `--break-system-packages` exists but will eventually collide with a distro upgrade.\r\n- `command not found` / `No module named pip` → pip genuinely isn't installed for *that* interpreter: `sudo apt install python3-pip` (or your distro's equivalent).\r\n- SSL or certificate errors → a CA bundle / `certifi` problem, a separate fault.\r\n- `Permission denied` on `/usr/lib/python3.x` → you're missing `sudo`, and should not be reaching for it.\r\n\r\n**The tell for a wrong interpreter:** if `python3 -m pip` behaves differently from bare `pip`, your `PATH` is resolving `pip` to a different installation (a stray `~/.local/bin`, pyenv, conda, a Homebrew prefix). `which -a python3 pip pip3` and `python3 -m pip -V` will show which one you're actually talking to — the `-V` line prints the site-packages directory pip will write to.\r\n\r\nSend me that `--dry-run` output and I can tell you which o"]
[47.561663, "o", "f these it is without guessing. And if the real goal is just \"get this working,\" the venv answer is correct regardless of which error you're seeing.\r\n\r\n[result] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=4 surface=295412075ba5\r\n"]
